---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const decisions = [
	{
		title: 'Pages on a CDN, the API on a laptop',
		text: 'The pages are plain files on GitHub Pages. Only the small API will run on the laptop at home.',
		pro: 'The portfolio stays up when the power or the internet goes out at home. Only the API goes down with it.',
		con: 'Two places to operate instead of one, and the API has to allow requests from another origin.',
	},
	{
		title: 'A static site, not server rendering',
		text: 'Pages are built ahead of time and served as plain files, so production has no Node process.',
		pro: 'Fast pages and very little to run. The site stays up even if the API goes down.',
		con: 'Any content change needs a new build and deployment.',
	},
	{
		title: 'SQLite, not Postgres',
		text: 'The only data will be contact messages, stored in a database file used by this site alone.',
		pro: 'No database server competing for 2 GB of RAM.',
		con: 'One writer at a time and a single machine. Backups need care, because copying a live file is not safe.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Cloudflare Tunnel, not open ports',
		text: 'A connector inside the laptop opens the connection outward, and Cloudflare handles HTTPS for the API.',
		pro: 'No open ports on my home network and no need for a public IP.',
		con: 'The API depends on Cloudflare, which also sits in the path of all its traffic.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Build in CI, never on the server',
		text: 'GitHub Actions runs the checks and builds everything. Nothing is published unless the checks pass.',
		pro: 'A slow laptop never compiles anything, and what runs is what was tested.',
		con: 'Publishing depends on GitHub, which now hosts the code, the CI and the pages.',
	},
	{
		title: 'What it costs',
		text: 'Nothing so far. A domain will be the first expense.',
		pro: 'The hardware already existed and the services used have free tiers.',
		con: 'I am the operations team, so every failure is mine to fix.',
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="About this site"
	description="How this site is built: static pages on a CDN, a small API on a 2014 laptop, and the trade-offs behind each decision."
>
	<h1>The pages come from a CDN. The API will come from a 2014 laptop.</h1>
	<p class="lead">
		The laptop is an ASUS X200MA with a Celeron N2830, 2 GB of RAM and a 500 GB hard drive.
	</p>

	<p style="margin-top: 2.5rem">
		This site is a working example of how I think about software: start from the real constraints, choose the
		simplest thing that fits and be honest about what each choice costs. The static pages went to a free host,
		because a laptop at home is a poor place for the part a recruiter opens first. The backend goes on the
		laptop, where its limits are worth showing.
	</p>

	<h2>How a request gets here</h2>
	<ol class="flow">
		<li>Your browser asks GitHub Pages for this page, and its CDN sends back a prebuilt file.</li>
		<li>
			When the API is live, requests for it go to Cloudflare over HTTPS and travel through a tunnel to the
			laptop.
		</li>
		<li>On the laptop, Caddy passes them to FastAPI, which stores contact messages in SQLite.</li>
	</ol>

	<h2>The decisions</h2>
	{
		decisions.map(({ title, text, pro, con }) => (
			<section>
				<h3>{title}</h3>
				<p>{text}</p>
				<ul class="tradeoffs">
					<li>
						<strong>Pro</strong> {pro}
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Con</strong> {con}
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>
		))
	}
</Layout>
